  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,\u2713$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
